pySMESH: netgen ignores the NGPROFILE environment variable (netgen 6.2.2101).

Why: ngcore's profiler (core/profiler.cpp) has one static NgProfiler, whose destructor
writes a timing table to "netgen.prof" in the working directory when NGPROFILE is set
(at process exit, since the profiler is static). pySMESH runs inside a host process: an
environment variable of that process must not make pySMESH write a file into the host's
working directory (tests/test_mesher_netgen_containment.py).

Change, in libsrc/core/profiler.cpp only: the NGPROFILE branch of ~NgProfiler is gone. The
branch that writes the profile to NgProfiler::filename stays; only netgen's Python module
sets that name, and pySMESH does not build it. No mesh changes.

diff --git a/libsrc/core/profiler.cpp b/libsrc/core/profiler.cpp
--- a/libsrc/core/profiler.cpp
+++ b/libsrc/core/profiler.cpp
@@ -35,17 +35,8 @@
         fclose(prof); // NOLINT
       }
 
-    if (getenv ("NGPROFILE"))
-      {
-       std::string filename = "netgen.prof";
-#ifdef PARALLEL
-       filename += "."+ToString(id);
-#endif
-       if (id == 0) logger->info( "write profile to file {}", filename );
-       FILE *prof = fopen(filename.c_str(),"w"); // NOLINT
-       Print (prof);
-       fclose(prof); // NOLINT
-      }
+    // pySMESH: NGPROFILE is not read. It made the process write netgen.prof into its
+    // working directory at exit, and pySMESH writes no file there.
   }
 
   void NgProfiler :: Print (FILE * prof)
